WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.346
Rosa asks whether her disability benefits will be taxed.

00:00:03.346 --> 00:00:06.319
The answer to almost every health insurance tax

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:08.922
question is the same: follow the premium.

00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:11.895
If the premium was paid with after-tax dollars

00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:14.497
and never deducted, the benefit is tax-free.

00:00:14.497 --> 00:00:18.587
If the premium was deducted, excluded from income or paid by

00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:22.676
an employer, the benefit is taxable, except where Congress made a

00:00:22.676 --> 00:00:26.021
specific exception, as it did for medical expense reimbursements.

00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:28.623
Learn that rule, then learn the exceptions.

00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:33.828
For individually owned coverage the premium is almost always after-tax, so benefits are almost

00:00:33.828 --> 00:00:38.660
always tax-free; the question is only whether the premium itself can be deducted.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.870
Rosa pays her own disability premium.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.657
She may not deduct it; disability income insurance is not a medical expense

00:00:45.657 --> 00:00:50.076
for tax purposes, because it replaces income rather than paying for care.

00:00:50.076 --> 00:00:53.022
In exchange, every dollar of benefit she receives

00:00:53.022 --> 00:00:55.969
while disabled is free of federal income tax.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:00.388
That is why insurers cap benefits at 60 to 70 percent of

00:01:00.388 --> 00:01:04.439
gross income: tax-free benefits at that level approach her take-home pay.

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:09.226
If Rosa later deducts the premium as a business expense, which the law

00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:13.646
does not permit for her own coverage, the benefits would become taxable.

00:01:13.646 --> 00:01:16.960
Keep the premium after-tax and the benefits stay clean.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.360
Marcus buys an individual medical plan.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.360
Its premiums are a medical expense, deductible only if he

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.360
itemizes and only to the extent his total unreimbursed medical

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.360
expenses, including premiums, exceed 7.5 percent of adjusted gross income.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.960
Most people never clear that floor, so most individual medical premiums produce no deduction.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.360
Benefits are different: amounts the plan pays to doctors and hospitals,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.760
or reimburses to Marcus for medical care, are never taxable income,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.760
even in the rare case where he deducted the premium.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.160
Premiums paid through the Exchange with a premium tax credit are

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.160
reconciled on the return; the credit itself is not income.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.542
Gloria’s tax-qualified long-term care premium is also a medical expense, but with

00:02:02.542 --> 00:02:06.923
a twist: only an age-based portion counts, indexed each year and rising

00:02:06.923 --> 00:02:11.305
from a few hundred dollars for people under 40 to several thousand

00:02:11.305 --> 00:02:15.322
for those over 70, and the 7.5 percent floor still applies.

00:02:15.322 --> 00:02:19.703
Benefits from a qualified policy are tax-free when they reimburse actual costs,

00:02:19.703 --> 00:02:24.085
and indemnity benefits are tax-free up to a daily per diem limit,

00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:28.467
indexed yearly, with amounts above it taxable unless they match actual expenses.

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:31.388
Maryland adds its own credit of up to

00:02:31.388 --> 00:02:33.944
500 dollars per insured, from Lesson 48.

00:02:33.944 --> 00:02:36.500
Nonqualified policies carry no assured federal treatment.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:42.456
A health savings account is the most tax-favored of the three accounts.

00:02:42.456 --> 00:02:47.419
Rosa’s contributions are deductible, or excluded from pay if made

00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:52.382
through payroll; earnings grow untaxed; withdrawals for qualified medical expenses

00:02:52.382 --> 00:02:56.849
are tax-free; and the balance is hers for life.

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:01.812
Nonqualified withdrawals are taxed and, before 65, penalized 20 percent.

00:03:01.812 --> 00:03:06.775
A health reimbursement arrangement is funded only by the employer,

00:03:06.775 --> 00:03:11.738
whose contributions are deductible business expenses and are not income

00:03:11.738 --> 00:03:16.205
to the employee; reimbursements for medical expenses are tax-free.

00:03:16.205 --> 00:03:20.176
The employee contributes nothing, so there is no

00:03:20.176 --> 00:03:23.650
deduction to claim and nothing to penalize.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.070
Marcus’s employer offers a health flexible spending account.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:31.773
He elects to reduce his salary by up to the annual

00:03:31.773 --> 00:03:36.476
limit, 3,400 dollars for 2026, and that money escapes income and

00:03:36.476 --> 00:03:41.179
payroll taxes; reimbursements of medical, dental and vision expenses are tax-free.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:45.882
The catch is use-it-or-lose-it: unused funds are forfeited at year end

00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:50.585
unless the plan allows a limited carryover or a grace period.

00:03:50.585 --> 00:03:56.143
A high-deductible health plan is simply a medical plan whose premiums follow the

00:03:56.143 --> 00:04:01.702
medical expense rules above; its tax significance is that it unlocks HSA eligibility.

00:04:01.702 --> 00:04:06.405
Compare the three: the HSA belongs to the individual, the HRA

00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:10.680
to the employer, and the FSA to the plan year.
